window.docContents[104].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Logarithms":{"contents":"\\frac{d}{dz}\\ln z=\\frac1z.$$For the principal logarithm, the derivative is 1 / z on Mathlib's slit plane,\nwhich excludes the branch cut and the origin.\n\n\\frac{d}{dz}\\mathrm{Ln}\\,z=\\frac1z.$$Every selected branch of the general logarithm has the same local derivative;\nthe Lean statement records the branch through its HasDerivAt hypothesis.\n\n\\frac{d^n}{dz^n}\\ln z=(-1)^{n-1}(n-1)!z^{-n}.$$For a positive integer n, repeated differentiation gives the displayed\nfactorial coefficient and negative power.\n\n\\frac{d^n}{dz^n}\\mathrm{Ln}\\,z=(-1)^{n-1}(n-1)!z^{-n}.$$The same higher-derivative formula holds for a general branch wherever its\nfirst derivative is 1 / z; this is a branch-explicit specialization.\n\n\\frac{dw}{dz}=\\frac{f'(z)}{f(z)}.$$For nonvanishing analytic f, this is the logarithmic differential equation\napplied to a candidate w on an explicit domain U; the Lean API records the\nequation as a reusable HasDerivAt predicate rather than asserting it for an\narbitrary function.\n\nw(z)=\\mathrm{Ln}(f(z))+\\text{constant}.$$On an open connected domain, if w and the chosen logarithm branch of f have\nthe same derivative, their difference is one additive constant.  This is a\nlocal-domain statement, as in DLMF.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.7 Derivatives and Differential Equations\u0009§4.7 Derivatives and Differential Equations","header":"§4.7(i) Logarithms","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7-Derivatives-and-Differential-Equations--___4___7_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Logarithms"},"/Gamma-Function/___5___7-Series-Expansions/#chapter-5-section-7":{"contents":"This page covers every numbered formula in\nDLMF §5.7. Infinite series are stated with HasSum, so convergence\nand the claimed value are one assertion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Gamma Function","header":"5.7. §5.7 Series Expansions","id":"/Gamma-Function/___5___7-Series-Expansions/#chapter-5-section-7"}});